@page "/localization"
@using System.Globalization
@inject IBaletteService Palette
@inject DemoState State

<PageTitle>Localization and RTL · Balette</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Localization &amp; RTL</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Every label and announcement of the palette - the ones only screen readers hear included - is a property of
        <code>BaletteStrings</code>, and every rule of its stylesheet uses logical properties, so a palette in German or in
        Persian, right to left, is a matter of handing it other strings and setting <code>dir</code>. The palette below
        switches language as you click.
    </p>
</div>

<PaletteBar />

<div class="bb-demo">
    <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">One palette, three languages</span></div>
    <div class="bb-segmented" role="group" aria-label="Palette language">
        @foreach (var language in Languages)
        {
            <button type="button" lang="@language.Code" aria-pressed="@(_language == language ? "true" : "false")" @onclick="() => SetLanguage(language)">@language.Name</button>
        }
    </div>
    <p>
        Its footer hints, placeholder, empty state, confirmation, disabled reason, the back button, the switch's spoken
        <em>@_language.Strings.On</em> and <em>@_language.Strings.Off</em>, and the word between the keys of
        <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd> all come from <code>BaletteStrings</code>. Type <code>xyz</code> for the empty state, open
        the export page, or arm the destructive row. The titles are translated too, with the English title as a keyword: type
        <code>settings</code> or <code>dark</code> in any of the three.
    </p>
    <div class="demo-inline-palette" dir="@(_language.Rtl ? "rtl" : "ltr")" lang="@_language.Code">
        <Balette Inline="true"
                 IncludeRegisteredCommands="false"
                 EnableHistory="false"
                 Strings="_language.Strings"
                 Style="--bit-balette-max-height: 260px"
                 Commands="_commands" />
    </div>
    <p class="bb-muted demo-small">
        The ids are the same in every language (<code>l10n:settings</code>, <code>l10n:export</code>…), so the active row and a
        page that is open survive a switch - and recents and pins would too.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Handing the palette its strings</h3>
    <p>
        Set <code>options.Strings</code> for every palette, or the palette's <code>Strings</code> parameter for one - the
        palette above uses the parameter. <code>BaletteShortcutHint</code> takes a <code>Strings</code> parameter as well,
        for the word between the chords of a sequence.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@OptionsExample" />
    <CodeBlock Code="@ParameterExample" />
    <p>
        Every property is <code>virtual</code>, so a subclass can read them from an <code>IStringLocalizer</code> - the usual
        resource files - instead:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@LocalizerExample" />
    <CodeBlock Code="@LocalizerMarkupExample" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Counts and plural forms</h3>
    <p>
        The result count is announced after every search. By default <code>ResultsAnnouncementFor(count)</code> picks
        <code>NoResultsAnnouncement</code>, <code>OneResultAnnouncement</code> or <code>ResultsAnnouncement</code>; a
        language whose plural forms one template per count cannot express overrides it. Polish has three:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@PluralExample" />
    <p>
        The Persian strings above override it too, to announce the count in Persian digits
        (<code>@PersianDigits(12)</code>) - a screen reader reading Persian expects them.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Translated commands</h3>
    <p>
        When titles are translated, add the untranslated title as a keyword - <code>.Keywords("Open settings")</code> on
        <em>Einstellungen öffnen</em> - so people who learned the English name still find it, as VS Code does with its English
        aliases. Give translated commands explicit ids, too: an id derived from the title would change with the language,
        and recents, pins and the active row with it.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@CommandsExample" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card" id="rtl">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Right to left</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Every rule uses logical properties, so <code>dir="rtl"</code> on the palette or anything around it lays it out
            from the right: the footer's last hint, the detail pane's border and the switch's knob all follow, and arrows and
            chevrons are mirrored.</li>
        <li>User text - titles, subtitles, badges, values, breadcrumbs, headings - isolates its own direction
            (<code>dir="auto"</code>), so an English title in a Persian UI keeps its punctuation where it belongs. Shortcut key
            caps always read left to right, as on the keys.</li>
        <li><kbd>←</kbd> and <kbd>→</kbd> trade places: <kbd>→</kbd> points back to the start of the line, so in
            right-to-left text it goes back a page, and <kbd>←</kbd> on an empty box opens the action panel.</li>
        <li>Search folds the Arabic and Persian forms of a letter and Persian digits (see <a href="search#folding">Search &amp;
            ranking</a>), and a shortcut such as <kbd>G</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd> works when a Persian layout types
            <code>ل</code> <code>س</code> (see <a href="keyboard#layouts">Keyboard</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The palette at the top of this page turns around with the language it is handed:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@RtlExample" />
    <p>
        This site's own palette goes right to left with the page: pick <em>Language › فارسی</em> or <em>العربية</em> in it, and
        the whole site turns around - the palette included.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick='() => Palette.OpenAsync(pageCommandId: "preferences.language")'>Open the Language page</button>
    </div>
    <p class="bb-muted demo-small" role="status">This site's language is <strong>@State.Language</strong>.</p>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Every string</h3>
    <p>
        Every property of <code>BaletteStrings</code>, with the library's English and, beside it, the language picked above.
        <code>{0}</code> and <code>{1}</code> are filled in with <code>string.Format</code> in the current culture.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Property</th><th>Where</th><th>English</th>@if (_language != Languages[0]) { <th lang="@_language.Code">@_language.Name</th> }</tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var (name, shownIn, read) in AllStrings)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td class="bb-table-key"><code>@name</code></td>
                        <td>@shownIn</td>
                        <td>@read(Languages[0].Strings)</td>
                        @if (_language != Languages[0])
                        {
                            <td lang="@_language.Code" dir="auto">@read(_language.Strings)</td>
                        }
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

